# Line endings, pinned only where a byte matters (T-719).
#
# Deliberately NOT `* text=auto`. This repository has files with both endings today and a
# blanket normalisation would rewrite hundreds of them in one commit, which is a change with
# a large blast radius and no benefit to most of them. What follows is the set where a CR is
# not cosmetic — either something parses the file at runtime, or something hashes it.
#
# The trigger was NFR-SEC-11: the Content-Security-Policy pins a sha256 of the pre-paint
# theme script inside `frontend/index.html` (R-58(1)). That hash is over the exact bytes
# between `<script>` and `</script>`, so a checkout that turned those newlines into CRLF
# would change it, the browser would refuse to run the script, and a user with a stored
# `light` preference would get a dark flash on every cold load — with nothing in any log.
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set on at least one development machine, so this is reachable
# rather than theoretical.
#
# The shell scripts and the clamd/nginx configuration are the same class of problem one
# layer down, and were already known to bite: a CR reaches `clamd.conf` and clamd blames
# line 1 for a stray carriage return anywhere in the file, and a CR in a `.sh` entrypoint
# makes a Linux container fail with the uninformative `bad interpreter: no such file`.
#
# Blast radius, measured rather than assumed (`git ls-files --eol`): 14 tracked files match
# these rules, the index already stores every one of them as LF, and `git add --renormalize`
# stages no content change. Two working-tree copies had drifted to CRLF under autocrlf
# (`deployment/docker-compose.yml`, `deployment/ocr/Dockerfile`) and normalise on the next
# checkout. So this file changes nothing that is committed today; it stops a future clone
# from differing.

# Hashed by the CSP, and parsed by a browser.
*.html      text eol=lf

# Executed by a shell inside a Linux container.
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Parsed by servers that are unforgiving about a stray CR.
*.conf      text eol=lf
*.inc       text eol=lf

# Read by Docker and by compose, in Linux containers.
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
